  "emi-calculator-bd": {
    title: "EMI Calculator Bangladesh (Loan Instalment Calculator)",
    desc: "Work out the monthly EMI, total interest and total repayment for home, personal, car or SME loans. Reducing balance and flat rate, processing fee, interest saved by paying extra, and the full repayment schedule.",
    howTo: ["Pick a loan type above, or keep ‘Custom’", "Enter the loan amount, your bank's annual interest rate and the tenure, or drag the sliders", "Choose reducing balance or flat rate", "Optionally add a processing fee and an extra monthly payment under ‘More options’", "Read the monthly EMI, total interest, chart and the yearly or monthly schedule, and download a CSV if you need it"],
    faqs: [
      { q: "What is EMI and how is it calculated?", a: "EMI (Equated Monthly Instalment) is the fixed amount you pay each month to repay a loan, and it contains both principal and interest. With the reducing balance method the formula is: EMI = P × r × (1+r)^n ÷ ((1+r)^n − 1), where P is the loan amount, r is the monthly rate (annual rate ÷ 12 ÷ 100) and n is the number of months." },
      { q: "What is the EMI on a 5 lakh taka loan for 5 years?", a: "At 10% a year (reducing balance), a 5 year loan of 5,00,000 taka has a monthly EMI of about 10,624 taka. The total repayment is 6,37,411 taka, so the interest is 1,37,411 taka." },
      { q: "What is the EMI on a 10 lakh taka loan?", a: "For 10 lakh taka at 10% over 5 years the EMI is about 21,247 taka and the total interest is 2,74,823 taka. At 12% over 3 years the EMI is about 33,214 taka and the total interest is 1,95,715 taka." },
      { q: "How do I calculate a home loan EMI?", a: "Pick ‘Home loan’ above and enter your bank's rate. Example: 50 lakh taka at 10% over 20 years gives an EMI of about 48,251 taka and total interest of 65,80,260 taka. On a long tenure the interest can exceed the amount borrowed, so check the total interest before choosing the tenure." },
      { q: "What is the difference between flat rate and reducing balance?", a: "With a flat rate, interest is charged on the original amount for the whole tenure; with reducing balance it is charged on the balance left each month. So at the same rate the flat EMI is higher. Example: 5,00,000 taka for 3 years at 10% flat has an EMI of about 18,056 taka, which equals about 17.92% on a reducing balance basis. At 10% reducing the EMI would be about 16,134 taka. Ask your lender which method applies before you borrow." },
      { q: "Does a longer tenure lower the EMI?", a: "Yes. The monthly EMI falls but the total interest rises. The ‘EMI at other tenures’ table shows the EMI and total interest for 1 to 20 years side by side so you can pick the balance that suits you." },
      { q: "How much interest do I save by paying extra every month?", a: "Enter an extra amount under ‘More options’ (reducing balance only) and the tool shows how many months earlier the loan ends and how much interest you save. Example: on a 10 lakh taka loan at 10% over 5 years, paying 5,000 taka extra every month clears the loan 13 months earlier and saves about 66,690 taka in interest. Check first whether your bank charges a fee for early repayment." },
      { q: "How do I add a processing fee?", a: "Enter the fee percentage under ‘More options’. The tool shows the fee in taka and the total cost including the fee. Check your bank's schedule of charges for the exact percentage." },
      { q: "Can I calculate a credit card or phone EMI here?", a: "Yes. Enter the price as the loan amount, the number of instalments as months and the interest rate. If the bank or seller offers zero interest, enter 0, and put any fee in the processing fee box. Rules differ by bank and offer, so take the final figure from your bank." },
      { q: "What is the difference between a fixed and a floating rate?", a: "With a fixed rate the interest stays the same for the whole tenure. With a floating rate the EMI or the tenure can change when the bank changes its rate. This tool uses one steady rate, so recalculate with the new rate if it changes." },
      { q: "Will the result match my bank exactly?", a: "Small differences can come from processing fees, insurance, excise duty or a different calculation method. You can enter the rate announced by BRAC Bank, Dutch-Bangla Bank or any other bank to get an estimate, but confirm the final figure with the bank." },
      { q: "Is the data I enter stored anywhere?", a: "No. Every calculation runs on your phone or in your browser, and nothing you enter is sent to a server or stored." },
    ],
  },
